---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar, line } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const gained = assertion('the-county-gained-households-and-lost-people')
const size = assertion('household-size-fell-a-quarter-with-no-reversal')
const doors = assertion('ten-thousand-dwellings-nobody-arrived-for')
const decades = assertion('almost-all-the-household-growth-is-one-decade')
const married = assertion('married-couple-households-peaked-in-1980')
const alone = assertion('one-person-households-more-than-doubled')
const ratio = assertion('four-and-a-quarter-to-one-became-one-and-a-half')
const occupied = assertion('a-household-and-an-occupied-unit-are-one-number')
const volumes = assertion('three-printed-volumes-check-the-compendium')
const thousands = assertion('one-cell-of-that-table-is-in-thousands')
const part = assertion('the-survey-and-the-census-part-company-by-a-ninth')
const third = assertion('a-third-of-this-countys-households-are-one-person')
const rank = assertion('seventh-of-eighty-eight-and-eighty-first')
const putnam = assertion('putnam-is-lowest-in-ohio-and-allen-is-fourteenth')
const outside = assertion('the-city-does-not-account-for-all-of-it')
const range = assertion('from-seventy-per-cent-to-twenty-six')
const closes = assertion('seven-columns-thirteen-rows-no-residual')
const tenure = assertion('in-lima-a-rented-house-holds-more-people')
const lone = assertion('lone-parents-outnumber-married-couples-in-lima')
const poor = assertion('six-times-as-likely-to-be-poor')
const poorFamilies = assertion('two-thirds-of-poor-families-and-a-fifth-of-families')
const grandparents = assertion('grandparents-raising-grandchildren')
const generations = assertion('thirteen-hundred-households-of-three-generations')
const samesex = assertion('the-first-count-of-same-sex-couples')
const marital = assertion('fewer-than-half-the-countys-adults-are-married')
const divorced = assertion('more-divorced-than-the-state-and-the-nation')
const never = assertion('half-the-men-in-lima-have-never-married')
const asymmetry = assertion('more-married-men-than-married-women')

const marriedSpec = line(
  married.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  [{ name: 'Married-couple households', y: married.figures.map((f) => f.value) }],
  { yTitle: 'Households', xTitle: 'Census', labelLast: true },
)
const doorsSpec = bar(
  doors.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  doors.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Dwellings', labelValues: true },
)
const tenureSpec = bar(
  tenure.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  tenure.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'People per household', labelValues: true },
)
const poorSpec = bar(
  poor.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  poor.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent below the poverty line', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="seven-thousand-more-front-doors">
  <p>
    The population of Allen County is the best-documented thing about it. There is a count for every
    decade from 1830, an estimate for every year since 1970, a period named for the decline and two
    files that say how much of the decline is people dying and how much is people leaving. What none
    of that says is how many places there are to live in, or how many people are in each of them —
    and those two numbers do not move the way the population does. One of them moves the other way.
  </p>

  <h2>Seven thousand more front doors</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={gained} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={size} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="doors"
    title="What household size did to the housing demand"
    caption="The middle bar is the county's actual household count in 2020. The first is what the same people would have needed at the household size of 1970."
    columns={['Measure', 'Dwellings']}
    rows={doors.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="doors" set:html={JSON.stringify(doorsSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={doors} />
  <p>
    That is the whole arithmetic of a shrinking county with a growing housing stock, and it needs no
    explanation beyond itself. Fewer people in more, smaller households need more dwellings. The
    building permits this site already counts are the record of those dwellings being built, and they
    were not built for newcomers.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={decades} />

  <h2>What the households became</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={married} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="married"
    title="Married-couple households in Allen County"
    caption="Six censuses. The county's household total rose 4.5 per cent between the first of these peaks and the last point on this line."
    columns={['Census', 'Households']}
    rows={married.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="married" set:html={JSON.stringify(marriedSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={alone} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ratio} />
  <p>
    A county of 34,000 households in 1970 was a county of married couples, seven in ten of them; a
    county of 41,000 in 2020 is a county in which nearly a third of the doors open on one person.
    Nothing in the population series shows that, because the population series has one number a
    decade and this has six.
  </p>

  <h2>How the figures were checked</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={occupied} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={volumes} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={thousands} />
  <p>
    One cell in one federal compendium is printed in thousands with a decimal point, in a table where
    every other cell is a count, and the only thing that says so is a reference file in a different
    workbook. Read straight, it makes a county of twenty-eight family households.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={part} />

  <h2>A third of the doors</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={third} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={rank} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={putnam} />
  <p>
    Putnam County begins at Allen County's north-western line. On the share of households headed by a
    woman with no spouse present it is last of Ohio's eighty-eight and Allen is fourteenth from the
    top. Two counties, one boundary, and a difference of eight points.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={outside} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={range} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={closes} />

  <h2>Inside the houses</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={tenure} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="tenure"
    title="Average household size by tenure"
    caption="Owned homes hold more people than rented ones everywhere except Lima, where the order is reversed by a third of a person."
    columns={['Geography and tenure', 'People']}
    rows={tenure.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="tenure" set:html={JSON.stringify(tenureSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The site already knows that renting in Allen County mostly means renting a house rather than a
    flat. This is the other half of it: the houses being rented in Lima are family-sized, and the
    ones being owned are increasingly held by one or two people who have been in them a long time.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lone} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={poor} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="poor"
    title="Families with children below the poverty line"
    caption="1,355 of 3,828 female-headed families with children, against 370 of 6,412 married-couple families with children."
    columns={['Family type', 'Per cent poor']}
    rows={poor.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="poor" set:html={JSON.stringify(poorSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={poorFamilies} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={grandparents} />

  <h2>Two things the census counts and the survey does not</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={generations} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={samesex} />
  <p>
    Both of those come from the 2020 census rather than from the survey, and for opposite reasons.
    The survey asks about three-generation households and publishes the answer for the nation and the
    states only; it does not ask about the sex of a householder's spouse in a way that yields this
    table at all. A file's reach is a property of each table in it.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is married</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={marital} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={divorced} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={never} />
  <p>
    Half the adult men in Lima have never been married. That is a fact about a city whose household
    structure sits closer to Cleveland's than to the townships that surround it, and it is the same fact as the married-couple share of its
    households, seen from the person's end rather than the address's.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={asymmetry} />
  <p>
    The last of those is a fact about the instrument. A set of couples has as many spouses on one
    side as the other, and the survey reports nearly two million more married men than married women
    in the United States. In a county this size the same subtraction disappears into the margin — the
    error is real, national, and invisible from here.
  </p>
</Article>
